	<h1>About</h1>
	<p>
		I split my time between stages and scripts, though rarely in the order you'd expect.
	</p>
	<p>
		Since 2011 I've been improvising: co-founding Swaajp Improtheater and its improv school in
		Antwerp, and performing with a handful of ensembles — Commotie, Prism, and Werewolves — that
		between them have picked up a Best Longform Improv Show award in Flanders and the Kemphanen
		Flemish improv championship more than once. I've taken the work on the road too: 250+ shows
		across ten countries and four languages, plus a spot at the 2024 European Championships for
		Improvisational Theatre. I also help build the wider scene — teaching at the Swaajp Improschool
		and the Mechelen Conservatory, and co-founding The SIN, a network connecting improvisers across
		Europe. Give me the chance and I'll grab a drum and play along from the side of the stage, too.
	</p>
	<p>
		Before any of that, I was a linguist. I finished a PhD in computational linguistics at the
		University of Antwerp in 2018, working on the AMiCA project — using text technology to detect
		harmful behaviour on social media — and on author profiling, the science of figuring out who
		wrote something from how they wrote it. It's a strange thing to have in common with improv, but
		both really come down to paying close attention to how people communicate.
	</p>
	<p>
		That combination is what led to <a href="https://www.erlnmyr.be" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
			>ERLNMYR</a
		>, the company I founded to put science on stage. Where a lecture usually runs in one
		direction, ERLNMYR turns it into something the audience takes part in — training, coaching,
		hosting, and full science-theatre shows, scaled anywhere from an academic conference hall down
		to a classroom of ten-year-olds. It's picked up some recognition along the way: third place at
		FameLab Belgium, the KVAB Science Communication Prize in 2017, and a slot at Bright Club
		Brussels. More recently that's expanded into workshops on using AI as part of science
		communication — as a subject, a tool, and occasionally a scene partner.
	</p>
	<p>
		If you're after a show, a workshop, or a keynote, that all runs through <a
			href="https://www.erlnmyr.be"
			target="_blank"
			rel="noopener noreferrer">ERLNMYR</a
		>. This site is more the place for the rest of it — writing, thinking out loud, and whatever
		else doesn't fit neatly on a business card.
	</p>
</PageLayout>
